{
  "format": "cegarplay",
  "version": 2,
  "title": "Minmax Clique in ASP(Q)",
  "description": "Minmax Clique (Ko and Lin 1995), a Pi-2-complete benchmark for ASP(Q) solvers, written level by level: for every pick t of one block per row (P1, forall) the picked blocks hold a clique of k vertices (P2, exists), and C is empty. The loop checks the dual, exists P1 forall P2 : false, so its solutions are the picks without such a clique: a candidate survives exactly when P2 has no answer set, vacuously. The property holds when the loop finds no solution (instance every-pick). The hand-written relevant/1 names the blocks of the clique, and the check prefers cliques over few rows (#minimize, an optimal solve), so the clauses are short; lift finds clauses as strong but takes far longer, and reference blocks. tools/make-aspq.py --qasp DIR writes the instances as qasp files for comparison.",
  "fragments": [
    {
      "name": "shared",
      "description": "The graph and its partition into blocks. Added to every solver.",
      "text": "% The graph (see the Instances tab): v(V,I,J) puts vertex V in block (I,J) of the\n% partition, e(U,V) is an edge (U < V); k is the clique size. Row I has the blocks (I,J).\nrow(I) :- v(_,I,_).\ncol(I,J) :- v(_,I,J).\nedge(U,V) :- e(U,V).\nedge(V,U) :- e(U,V).\n"
    },
    {
      "name": "p1",
      "description": "P1: one block per row (solver A).",
      "text": "% P1 (forall in the ASP(Q) program, exists in the loop): t picks one block per row.\n1 { t(I,J) : col(I,J) } 1 :- row(I).\n#show t/2.\n"
    },
    {
      "name": "glue",
      "description": "The interface atoms: the picks.",
      "text": "% The interface atoms: the picks of t.\nglue(t(I,J)) :- col(I,J).\n"
    },
    {
      "name": "p2",
      "description": "P2: a clique of k vertices in the picked blocks (solver C).",
      "text": "% P2 (exists in the ASP(Q) program): a clique of k vertices among the picked blocks.\n% C is empty, so the loop's check, forall P2 : false, is refuted by any answer set of P2:\n% a candidate t survives only when P2 has no answer set, i.e. vacuously.\n{ in(V) } :- v(V,I,J), t(I,J).\n:- in(U), in(V), U < V, not edge(U,V).\n:- #count { V : in(V) } != k.\n#show in/1.\n"
    },
    {
      "name": "relevant",
      "description": "The hand-written relevant/1: the blocks of the clique.",
      "text": "% The clique stays a clique as long as t keeps the blocks of its vertices: one of them must\n% change. The rows of the clique, not its vertices, name the atoms.\nused(I,J) :- in(V), v(V,I,J).\nrelevant(t(I,J)) :- used(I,J).\n"
    },
    {
      "name": "fewest",
      "description": "A clique over few rows first.",
      "text": "% Prefer cliques that use few rows: shorter clauses (with C.solve(..., optimal)).\n#minimize { 1,I : used(I,J) }.\n"
    }
  ],
  "loop": {
    "language": "loop",
    "text": "solver A = shared + p1\nsolver C = shared + p2 + relevant + fewest\nlink   G = A -> C via shared + glue\n\n# A candidate is a pick t; the check looks for a k-clique among the picked blocks, the one\n# that spans the fewest rows; the clause says: one of those rows picks another block.\nsearch A:\n    on candidate M:\n        X = C.solve(assume G(M), optimal)\n        if X: reject M with G.clause(M, X)\n"
  },
  "settings": {
    "constants": {
      "k": "3"
    },
    "solutions": 0,
    "seconds": 60,
    "candidates": 0,
    "trace": 200,
    "seed": 0
  },
